    "result_text": "Implementation complete and the suite is green. Here's what I did:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change emits an immutable structured audit event alongside the existing audit line whenever an owner is created.\n\n**1. `Owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`** (new accessor in `model/Owner.java`)\nThe single future-proofing hook. It returns the customer code today. Routing the event's identifier through *\"the primary identifier\"* rather than `getCustomerCode()` directly means that when the customer code is later unified into the member id, only this one method changes and the event's payload follows automatically.\n\n**2. `OwnerCreatedEvent`** (new record in `service/OwnerCreatedEvent.java`)\nAn immutable record `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}` \u2014 the field order matches the spec, and being a record makes it immutable once emitted. Its convenience constructor `(long seq, Owner owner)` pulls the identifier from `owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`, so the record itself expresses the \"carry the current primary identifier\" rule. The event type constant lives here.\n\n**3. `OwnerAuditor.ownerCreated()`** (updated in `service/OwnerAuditor.java`)\nKeeps the original human-readable audit line, then emits the structured event as JSON via a shared Jackson `ObjectMapper`. The monotonic `seq` is an `AtomicLong` on the singleton auditor, incremented once per create \u2014 so sequence assignment stays where audit emission already lives, rather than leaking into the controller or model.\n\nThe auditor remains the sole owner of audit side-effects, the event's data-shape lives in its own record, and the \"which field is the primary identifier\" decision lives solely in `Owner` \u2014 each responsibility in one small place.\n\nVerified output line:\n```\nINFO  AUDIT - {\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\n`AcceptanceTest` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
